const title = 'Pregnancy Test Timing';
const description = 'When to take a pregnancy test for the most accurate result — how tests work, early testing, and what to do with your result.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section section-white">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="prose">
    <h1>Pregnancy Test Timing: When to Test</h1>

    <p>Taking a pregnancy test at the right time can mean the difference between an accurate result and a false negative. This guide explains how pregnancy tests work, when to test, and what to do with your result.</p>

    <div class="affiliate-disclosure">
      <strong>Affiliate disclosure:</strong> This guide contains affiliate links. If you click an affiliate link and make a purchase, we may earn a commission at no additional cost to you. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">full disclosure</a>.
    </div>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>How Pregnancy Tests Work</h2>

    <p>Home pregnancy tests detect the hormone <strong>human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)</strong> in your urine. hCG is produced by the placenta after implantation.</p>

    <p>Most home tests can detect hCG at levels of 25 mIU/mL or lower. Some "early detection" tests can detect hCG at 10 mIU/mL.</p>

    <h2>When to Take a Pregnancy Test</h2>

    <h3>Best Time: After a Missed Period</h3>
    <p>For the most accurate result, take a pregnancy test <strong>after your period is late</strong>. By this time, hCG levels are usually high enough to detect.</p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />

    <h3>Early Testing</h3>
    <p>Some tests claim to detect pregnancy up to 6 days before a missed period. However, testing this early increases the risk of a false negative because hCG levels may not yet be high enough.</p>

    <p>If you test early and get a negative result, wait a few days and test again if your period has not started.</p>

    <h3>Best Time of Day</h3>
    <p>Use your <strong>first morning urine</strong>, which is most concentrated and contains the highest hCG levels. If you test later in the day, avoid drinking large amounts of fluids before testing.</p>

    <h2>What Can Affect Test Results</h2>

    <h3>False Negatives</h3>
    <p>A false negative (test says you are not pregnant, but you are) can happen if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You test too early</li>
      <li>Your urine is too diluted</li>
      <li>You do not follow the test instructions</li>
      <li>The test is expired or damaged</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>False Positives</h3>
    <p>A false positive (test says you are pregnant, but you are not) is rare but can happen if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You have recently been pregnant (hCG can remain in your body for weeks after a miscarriage or birth)</li>
      <li>You are taking fertility medications containing hCG</li>
      <li>You have a medical condition that produces hCG (rare)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What to Do with Your Result</h2>

    <h3>If the Test Is Positive</h3>
    <p>A positive result is almost always accurate. Contact your healthcare provider to schedule your first prenatal visit. Start taking a prenatal vitamin with folic acid if you are not already.</p>

    <h3>If the Test Is Negative</h3>
    <p>If your period is late and the test is negative:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Wait a few days and test again</li>
      <li>Use your first morning urine</li>
      <li>If your period is more than a week late, contact your healthcare provider</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You have a positive pregnancy test</li>
      <li>Your period is more than a week late and tests are negative</li>
      <li>You have irregular cycles and are unsure when to test</li>
      <li>You have symptoms of pregnancy but negative tests</li>
    </ul>

